WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.497
Welcome to Module 6 of the
Attribute-Driven Migration learning path:

2
00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:07.150
onboard servers with Ansible beside Arc.

3
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:11.062
This module serves Gates 5 and
8 of the implementation guide,

4
00:00:11.062 --> 00:00:15.287
and takes Chapter 08 of Building
the Attribute-Driven Migration.

5
00:00:15.287 --> 00:00:19.371
You'll onboard one wave of servers
to Azure Arc with Microsoft's role,

6
00:00:19.371 --> 00:00:22.507
from a CMDB export, with
every server tagged with the

7
00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:25.862
tool that owns its settings
and the tool that patches it.

8
00:00:25.862 --> 00:00:29.475
And you'll write a plan a reviewer
reads before anything runs.

9
00:00:29.475 --> 00:00:35.577
The lab reads the CMDB into groups and
tags, runs Microsoft's role in check mode,

10
00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:40.725
plans a wave, dry-runs it, and breaks
the CMDB on purpose, all offline.

11
00:00:40.725 --> 00:00:46.450
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not against a production estate.

12
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.350
Why does this matter?

13
00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:55.951
In this workbook's implementation guide,
Gate 5's server convention says an

14
00:00:55.951 --> 00:01:00.775
Arc server's tags are its attributes,
drawn from the asset record at onboarding.

15
00:01:00.775 --> 00:01:05.975
Gate 8 writes each wave as a query:
servers by resource group and tag.

16
00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:07.622
Microsoft's azure arc role,

17
00:01:07.622 --> 00:01:12.563
which Microsoft Learn documents in Connect
machines to Azure Arc at scale with

18
00:01:12.563 --> 00:01:16.756
Ansible, connects whatever hosts
the inventory names, with no waves,

19
00:01:16.756 --> 00:01:20.200
and with no dry run on a server
that doesn't have the agent.

20
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.225
And the tags are written once,
at the connect, from the CMDB.

21
00:01:24.225 --> 00:01:27.458
In this workbook's reading,
that makes the onboarding

22
00:01:27.458 --> 00:01:30.837
run the place where owners
are read, checked and carried.

23
00:01:30.837 --> 00:01:35.351
A server connected without them is open
to whichever tool reaches it first.

24
00:01:35.351 --> 00:01:39.498
In this module you read the CMDB
into groups and tags, plan a wave,

25
00:01:39.498 --> 00:01:44.090
and see what each server would
get before anything is contacted.

26
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.655
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

27
00:01:50.655 --> 00:01:53.278
which takes about forty-five
minutes with the lab.

28
00:01:53.278 --> 00:01:56.810
You'll explain why each server
records one configuration

29
00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.902
owner and one patch owner,
and where each tag is read.

30
00:01:59.902 --> 00:02:03.341
You'll describe what Microsoft's
azure arc role leaves to you.

31
00:02:03.341 --> 00:02:07.003
You'll run the CMDB inventory,
and say what makes it stop.

32
00:02:07.003 --> 00:02:10.790
You'll plan a wave and read
the plan, before anything runs.

33
00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:14.684
And you'll explain why the role can't
dry-run a server without the agent,

34
00:02:14.684 --> 00:02:16.665
and what the playbook does instead.

35
00:02:16.665 --> 00:02:18.864
Before you start, finish Module 5:

36
00:02:18.864 --> 00:02:24.077
its enforcing definitions filter on the
Config Owner tag this module writes.

37
00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:28.608
Lab 1 needs a disposable
Linux control node, or WSL,

38
00:02:28.608 --> 00:02:35.128
with Python 3.10 or later, ansible
core, ansible lint, yamllint and pytest.

39
00:02:35.128 --> 00:02:40.170
Step three runs Microsoft's role as
root against the control node itself.

40
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.470
This workbook's guidance gives
every setting one owning tool.

41
00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:50.698
The CMDB records it for
each asset as config owner,

42
00:02:50.698 --> 00:02:55.108
and the workbook's Arc track guide
mirrors it to the Config Owner tag.

43
00:02:55.108 --> 00:03:00.196
Patching has one owner too: a patch owner
column, mirrored to the Patch Owner tag.

44
00:03:00.196 --> 00:03:02.133
Arc doesn't change who owns what.

45
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:04.266
It makes the server an Azure resource,

46
00:03:04.266 --> 00:03:07.570
and the tags tell the Azure
tools which servers are theirs.

47
00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:12.434
The kit's enforcing machine configuration
definitions filter on Config Owner,

48
00:03:12.434 --> 00:03:16.045
so machine configuration changes
only the servers it owns.

49
00:03:16.045 --> 00:03:20.667
Microsoft Learn's page, About dynamic
scoping, for Azure Update Manager,

50
00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:26.073
says dynamic scopes group machines by tags
and are evaluated at each scheduled run,

51
00:03:26.073 --> 00:03:28.658
so a scope on Patch Owner follows the tag.

52
00:03:28.658 --> 00:03:35.058
Take aws oh one, a Red Hat server on
AWS that Ansible configures and patches.

53
00:03:35.058 --> 00:03:38.661
It still goes to Arc, with
Patch Owner set to Ansible, so,

54
00:03:38.661 --> 00:03:43.983
in this workbook's guidance, Update
Manager assesses it and schedules nothing.

55
00:03:43.983 --> 00:03:47.968
And afterward, the kit's check V
K ARC oh seven reports any server

56
00:03:47.968 --> 00:03:53.070
machine configuration doesn't own that
carries its enforcing assignment.

57
00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.208
One more piece of the
setting: which Azure.

58
00:03:59.208 --> 00:04:05.221
The estate this workbook plans
for is a Microsoft 365 GCC tenant.

59
00:04:05.221 --> 00:04:11.516
Microsoft Learn's page, Cloud feature
availability for US Government customers,

60
00:04:11.516 --> 00:04:16.308
says Office 365 GCC is paired
with Microsoft Entra ID in Azure.

61
00:04:16.308 --> 00:04:20.392
So a GCC estate's Arc servers go
to a commercial subscription, and,

62
00:04:20.392 --> 00:04:23.320
as Learn's azcmagent
connect reference shows,

63
00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.558
the agent connects with the AzureCloud
cloud, which is the default.

64
00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:30.507
The agent then needs the
commercial endpoints,

65
00:04:30.507 --> 00:04:35.724
which Learn's Connected Machine agent
network requirements list: the sign-in hosts,

66
00:04:35.724 --> 00:04:39.428
management dot azure dot com,
only to connect or disconnect,

67
00:04:39.428 --> 00:04:43.133
and the Arc and guest configuration
services, among others.

68
00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:45.182
The kit guards the cloud twice.

69
00:04:45.182 --> 00:04:49.683
The wave plan stops a server whose
region isn't a commercial Azure region,

70
00:04:49.683 --> 00:04:54.840
and the playbook that uses Microsoft's
role refuses any cloud but AzureCloud.

71
00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:03.125
Microsoft Learn's page, Connect machines
to Azure Arc at scale with Ansible,

72
00:05:03.125 --> 00:05:05.832
documents onboarding through the Azure Arc

73
00:05:05.832 --> 00:05:08.465
onboarding role in the
azure az collection.

74
00:05:08.465 --> 00:05:12.553
It installs the Connected Machine
agent and registers each host.

75
00:05:12.553 --> 00:05:16.883
The repository keeps a pinned copy
of the role's code, from the ansible

76
00:05:16.883 --> 00:05:21.214
collections azure repository at the
four point two point oh release.

77
00:05:21.214 --> 00:05:25.190
Running it offline, and reading its
code, shows what it leaves to you.

78
00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.272
It connects whatever
hosts the inventory names;

79
00:05:28.272 --> 00:05:31.740
the kit reads both owners and
writes them into the tags.

80
00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:36.615
It has no waves; the kit adds a wave
column, one canary server, then five,

81
00:05:36.615 --> 00:05:41.752
then twenty-five percent, and keeps
production out of waves zero and one.

82
00:05:41.752 --> 00:05:44.930
It can't run in check mode on
a server without the agent;

83
00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:48.252
the kit's playbook doesn't call
it without a confirm flag.

84
00:05:48.252 --> 00:05:52.670
The role's code sends a signal with
the subscription and tenant by default,

85
00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:56.002
which Learn's page doesn't
mention; the kit turns that off.

86
00:05:56.002 --> 00:05:58.711
And it leaves no record,
and the requirements

87
00:05:58.711 --> 00:06:01.640
file on Learn's page
installs the latest version.

88
00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.116
The verdict, which is this workbook's:

89
00:06:04.116 --> 00:06:08.440
use Microsoft's role, unchanged,
and add what sits around it.

90
00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:17.502
The kit's CMDB inventory script turns
each owner into a group and a tag.

91
00:06:17.502 --> 00:06:22.604
A config owner of arc machine configuration,
Ansible, Intune and so on becomes a

92
00:06:22.604 --> 00:06:27.627
group, and a Config Owner tag in the
spelling of this workbook's Arc track guide.

93
00:06:27.627 --> 00:06:29.590
The patch owner works the same way.

94
00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:33.897
An empty owner on a tier one or
two record goes to a missing group,

95
00:06:33.897 --> 00:06:37.789
which is a finding under NIST
special publication eight

96
00:06:37.789 --> 00:06:41.765
hundred fifty-three control
CM-8, inventory, never a target.

97
00:06:41.765 --> 00:06:43.715
And the wave becomes a wave group.

98
00:06:43.715 --> 00:06:45.515
Three rules fail closed.

99
00:06:45.515 --> 00:06:48.303
An owner value the script
doesn't know stops it.

100
00:06:48.303 --> 00:06:52.412
A typo like update manager written
as one word would otherwise create a

101
00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:56.590
group nothing reads, and drop the server
out of the group that protects it.

102
00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:58.640
A wave that isn't a number stops it.

103
00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.918
And a server is a candidate only if its
record has both owners, a resource group,

104
00:07:03.918 --> 00:07:06.722
a region, a wave, and
tag values Arc accepts;

105
00:07:06.722 --> 00:07:12.165
Learn's azcmagent connect reference takes
the tags as one comma-delimited list.

106
00:07:12.165 --> 00:07:15.090
Otherwise it's blocked,
with the reasons recorded.

107
00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.360
All three are the kit's design.

108
00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:24.710
The kit's wave plan playbook
runs only on the control node.

109
00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.073
You give it a wave number.

110
00:07:26.073 --> 00:07:30.984
It lists the wave's ready servers
and the ones the CMDB blocked,

111
00:07:30.984 --> 00:07:36.761
and writes a JSON and a CSV plan under
reports: host, configuration item,

112
00:07:36.761 --> 00:07:40.132
environment, resource
group, region, cloud,

113
00:07:40.132 --> 00:07:43.598
both owners, the exact
tag string and a state.

114
00:07:43.598 --> 00:07:47.301
It stops when no wave is given
or the wave has no ready server,

115
00:07:47.301 --> 00:07:51.382
when the cloud is wrong or a region
isn't a commercial Azure region,

116
00:07:51.382 --> 00:07:55.463
when a production server is planned
in wave zero or one, which this

117
00:07:55.463 --> 00:08:00.148
workbook's Arc track keeps for non-production,
and when a server has no owner.

118
00:08:00.148 --> 00:08:03.744
Both onboarding playbooks
import it as their first play,

119
00:08:03.744 --> 00:08:06.422
so if the plan stops,
no server is contacted.

120
00:08:06.422 --> 00:08:11.281
The playbook that uses Microsoft's role
runs in batches, one canary server,

121
00:08:11.281 --> 00:08:13.592
then five, then twenty-five percent,

122
00:08:13.592 --> 00:08:17.734
with the batch keywords the
Ansible documentation describes.

123
00:08:17.734 --> 00:08:20.960
It's in check mode unless you
set confirm change to true.

124
00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.735
Then the role isn't called, and each
server prints what it would get.

125
00:08:24.735 --> 00:08:27.808
With the flag, the role runs
with the CMDB's values,

126
00:08:27.808 --> 00:08:30.940
authenticating with the managed identity.

127
